1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,092
Lesson 1.7.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:03,489
A historical arc.

3
00:00:04,617 --> 00:00:07,076
The ideas of this course
were not born together.

4
00:00:07,456 --> 00:00:11,598
They come from three rivers that
ran separately for more than a

5
00:00:11,598 --> 00:00:16,069
century: the thermodynamics of steam
engines, the information theory

6
00:00:16,069 --> 00:00:20,014
of telecommunications, and
physics-inspired neural networks.

7
00:00:20,394 --> 00:00:25,163
In this lesson we walk the timeline
and see how those rivers merge.

8
00:00:26,283 --> 00:00:32,359
In 1824, Sadi Carnot asked how much
work a steam engine can deliver.

9
00:00:32,739 --> 00:00:37,715
His answer was universal: the maximum
efficiency between two reservoirs depends only

10
00:00:37,715 --> 00:00:41,921
on their temperatures, one minus the
cold temperature over the hot one.

11
00:00:42,301 --> 00:00:46,808
Clausius turned that intuition into
the second law and defined entropy.

12
00:00:47,188 --> 00:00:50,021
It was a science of engines,
without atoms or bits.

13
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:56,926
Ludwig Boltzmann, in the 1870s,
explained entropy by counting

14
00:00:56,926 --> 00:01:02,417
microstates: S = k ln W, the
formula engraved on his tomb.

15
00:01:02,797 --> 00:01:07,104
Josiah Willard Gibbs, in
1902, built statistical

16
00:01:07,104 --> 00:01:12,040
ensembles and the distribution
we use today, e^(−βE)/Z.

17
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:17,808
Thermodynamics became statistical:
probabilities over microscopic states.

18
00:01:18,917 --> 00:01:22,590
In 1948, Claude Shannon
defined information and

19
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:26,030
its entropy to design
communication systems.

20
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:28,159
The formula was Gibbs's.

21
00:01:28,539 --> 00:01:33,010
In 1961, Rolf Landauer
connected both worlds:

22
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:36,786
erasing a bit costs at least k T ln 2.

23
00:01:37,166 --> 00:01:40,569
And Charles Bennett, in the
following decades, showed that

24
00:01:40,569 --> 00:01:44,031
computation can be reversible
and resolved Maxwell's demon.

25
00:01:44,411 --> 00:01:46,230
Information became physical.

26
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:51,357
In 1982, John Hopfield showed
that a network of binary

27
00:01:51,357 --> 00:01:55,289
neurons with symmetric
connections is an Ising model:

28
00:01:55,289 --> 00:01:59,222
associative memory is
descending an energy landscape.

29
00:01:59,602 --> 00:02:03,866
In 1985, Geoffrey Hinton, with
Ackley and Sejnowski, added

30
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:08,056
temperature and sampling: the
Boltzmann machine learns by

31
00:02:08,056 --> 00:02:11,585
matching the model's
correlations to the data's.

32
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:16,556
In 2024 both received the Nobel
Prize in Physics for these ideas.

33
00:02:17,683 --> 00:02:22,203
In 1997, Christopher Jarzynski
discovered an exact equality:

34
00:02:22,203 --> 00:02:26,572
the average of the exponential
of minus the work gives the

35
00:02:26,572 --> 00:02:30,187
free-energy difference,
even for fast processes.

36
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:34,507
In 1999, Gavin Crooks
generalized it by relating the

37
00:02:34,507 --> 00:02:38,597
probabilities of a trajectory
and of its time reverse.

38
00:02:38,977 --> 00:02:43,450
Stochastic thermodynamics was
born: the thermodynamics of small,

39
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:47,783
noisy systems far from equilibrium,
like a thermodynamic chip.

40
00:02:48,883 --> 00:02:51,945
The three rivers merge in
thermodynamic computing.

41
00:02:52,325 --> 00:02:56,302
From thermodynamics it takes free
energy and efficiency limits.

42
00:02:56,682 --> 00:02:58,964
From information, the cost of erasing and

43
00:02:58,964 --> 00:03:01,245
the equivalence between bits and entropy.

44
00:03:01,625 --> 00:03:03,890
From neural networks, the idea that

45
00:03:03,890 --> 00:03:06,801
learning is fitting a
Boltzmann distribution.

46
00:03:07,181 --> 00:03:10,626
And from stochastic
thermodynamics, the tools to

47
00:03:10,626 --> 00:03:13,497
measure work and heat in a noisy device.

48
00:03:13,877 --> 00:03:15,802
That is the intellectual
map of the course.

49
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:19,821
Every name in this story
is a tool of the course.

50
00:03:20,201 --> 00:03:22,606
Carnot and Clausius give us the limits.

51
00:03:22,986 --> 00:03:26,814
Boltzmann and Gibbs, the program
a thermodynamic chip executes.

52
00:03:27,194 --> 00:03:30,763
Shannon, Landauer and Bennett,
the cost of information.

53
00:03:31,143 --> 00:03:34,925
Hopfield and Hinton, the
learning models of unit four.

54
00:03:35,305 --> 00:03:39,452
And Jarzynski and Crooks, the
way to extract free energies,

55
00:03:39,452 --> 00:03:43,177
and even matrix determinants,
from work measurements.

56
00:03:44,283 --> 00:03:45,737
Some myths to avoid.

57
00:03:46,117 --> 00:03:50,494
Shannon did not copy Gibbs: the formal
coincidence was recognized later.

58
00:03:50,874 --> 00:03:54,437
The demon was not resolved at
measurement, but at erasure.

59
00:03:54,817 --> 00:03:59,243
Boltzmann machines were not wrong: they
were expensive to sample on digital

60
00:03:59,243 --> 00:04:03,019
hardware, which is why thermodynamic
hardware could revive them.

61
00:04:03,399 --> 00:04:07,497
And the Jarzynski equality does not
contradict the second law: it contains it.

62
00:04:08,617 --> 00:04:12,304
In summary: thermodynamics
was born with engines, became

63
00:04:12,304 --> 00:04:16,123
statistical with Boltzmann and
Gibbs, met information with

64
00:04:16,123 --> 00:04:20,403
Shannon and Landauer, inspired
learning with Hopfield and Hinton,

65
00:04:20,783 --> 00:04:23,683
and became stochastic
with Jarzynski and Crooks.

66
00:04:24,063 --> 00:04:28,407
Thermodynamic computing is the
point where all those lines cross.

67
00:04:29,517 --> 00:04:32,474
To practice: compute a Carnot efficiency.

68
00:04:32,854 --> 00:04:35,321
Explain the formula on Boltzmann's tomb.

69
00:04:35,701 --> 00:04:37,294
Summarize Bennett's contribution.

70
00:04:37,674 --> 00:04:41,186
And derive the second law
from the Jarzynski equality.
